CONSEQUENCES OF REFERENDUMS:-


Are referendums legally binding?:-
- Many referendums held across the UK have resulted in notable political change. However, parliament always remains sovereign, so governments are not bound to follow the decision of the voters.
- This became a significant issue following the Brexit referendum. MPs such as Dominic Grieve claimed ‘the EU referendum was an advisory referendum.’

Why do governments face considerable political pressure to follow the results of referendums?:-
- However, the government is under considerable political pressure to follow the result of a referendum. Giving the public a choice and then ignoring the outcome casts questions of legitimacy and accountability of the government.

Why might ‘referendum thresholds’ help give results more [[legitimacy]]?:-
- Referendums in recent years have proven controversial. While it has been claimed that big issues such as Brexit and Scottish independence have been settled, the relatively close vote has meant that the controversy surrounded these issues remains. Would this have been solved through the use of the thresholds?
- A threshold could be set for a minimum turnout needed for a referendum result to be followed - for example, 50%
- A threshold could be set for the minimum vote needed for a result to be followed. This could be a supermajority such as 60% or two thirds

Why might ‘referendum thresholds’ not help give results legitimacy?:-
- If you insist on a turnout threshold turnout could lower, as not voting is effectively the same as a ‘no’ vote.
- People who will not vote will be counted as ‘no’ votes - this might not be their actual view.

What does it meant to say that the UK constitution is ‘uncodified and unentrenched’, and why might this lead to more referendums in future?:-
- Because the constitution is easy to change and many referendums are to do with these constitutional issues there will likely be more.

What reason did PM Theresa May give for ignoring calls for a second EU referendum in 2018-19:-
- Theresa May opposed that not delivering on the result of the 2016 referendum would ignore the democratic will of the people and threaten social cohesion.
 
What is the Burkean principle, and why does this mean referendums create tension between voters and their representatives?:-
- The UK’s representative democracy hinges on the ‘Burkean principle’ - elected officials being allowed to use their conscience to act in the best interests of their constituents on the basis of being fully informed.
- Direct democracy in the form of referendums causes tension in who knows best

