ARE REFERENDUMS GOOD OR BAD FOR DEMOCRACY:-

PARTICIPATION:-
GOOD:-
- They can encourage participation and education, as shown in the Scottish independence and Brexit referendums, which enhances legitimacy and consent in the political system
- Brexit turnout = 72.2%, IndyRef turnout = 84.6% 
- Provides another way in which the public can engage in politics between elections, and ensure a responsive government with a mandate to act
- e.g. 2010 election and then 2015 election and 2011 ATV referendum and 2014 IndyRef

BAD:-
- Turnout is often very low which undermines the legitimacy of the decision and the effectiveness of direct democracy
- e.g. London make a mayor election 1998 34% turnout or ATV referendum 42.2% turnout

REPRESENTATION:-
GOOD:-
- They have enhanced representation in the UK by allowing popular sovereignty to be directly expressed 
- e.g. popular sovereignty over parliamentary sovereignty around Brexit

BAD:-
- The issues raised are often too complex to reduce to a ‘yes’ or ‘no’ chose, with voters lacking the necessary information to make a fully informed decision
- e.g. difference between hard and soft Brexit that caused lots of controversy

ACCOUNTABILITY:- 
- They have enhanced liberal democracy by serving as a way to limit the power of the government 
- e.g. many MPs wanted Brexit but instead followed the referendum result 
- The management of referendums is overseen by the Electoral Commission in the same manner as elections. This limits government manipulation

BAD:- 
- The government decides if and when to call a referendum, which concentrates power in the government rather than limiting it
